// WHY: locks FRAGO 002 cluster C2 — `.copy()`'s per-type lowering used to have an
// `AliasNoOp` arm for `fixed<T>` that returned the RECEIVER'S OWN pointer instead of
// an independent copy. `b = a.copy()` therefore aliased `a`, and mutating `b` mutated
// `a`. No transfer, no diagnostic — a silent wrong answer in the default mode.
//
// Observed before the fix (2026-09-06, default optimized mode): printed "99"
// where "1" is correct (`a[0]` reads the mutation made through `b`). Confirmed-live
// by dispatch hardening-p2b-20260905-a1 as probe N
// (target/p2b-probe/n_copy_fixed.ynz, gitignored). Shares a design ancestor (not a
// code line) with M8 FR #9's bg-arg escape door — Patrick's 2026-09-06 ruling
// required EVERY type in that arm to become either a real deep copy or a compile-time
// refusal; `AliasNoOp` survives as neither. Closes M8 FR #10.
//
// FIXED by Phase 3 step 3.2: exit 0, stdout exactly "1" — `a` is untouched by
// mutating the copy. `fixed<T>` landed on the COPY side of the ruling rather than the
// refusal side, because its cells hold the items outright, so copying the cells into
// fresh storage genuinely separates the two lists. Silent aliasing is the wrong answer
// this fixture exists to catch, and a "99" here means it came back.
function entrypoint() -> nothing {
  let a: fixed<int> = [1, 2, 3]
  let b = a.copy()
  b.set(0, 99)
  print(a[0].or(-1).toString())
}
